<TopicPage slug="subjects/cognitive-science/foundational/what-is-cognitive-science">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Cognitive science is built on a single, enormous wager: that minds are systems which
    process information, and can therefore be studied the way other complex systems are —
    with models, measurements and experiments. When you read this sentence, light patterns
    on your retina become letters, letters become words, words become meaning; when you
    later recall it, something in your brain is retrieved, recombined and expressed. The
    wager says those events have <em>structure</em> — describable, modelled, tested — and
    that we do not have to choose between ignoring the mind's mystery and declaring it
    unknowable.
  </p>
  <p>
    No single discipline could cash that wager alone. Psychology measures what minds do
    (behaviour, reaction times, errors) but not how; neuroscience measures brains but
    drowns in detail without questions posed from above; linguistics reveals the structure
    of the mind's most characteristic product; philosophy keeps the concepts honest; and
    computer science builds the models that turn hunches into running, failing, testable
    theories. The field is the triangulation — literally the name for the method: fix the
    unknown mind by measuring it from several disciplinary angles at once, and let the
    lines cross.
  </p>
  <p>
    The wager had a history, a philosophy and a method, and this chapter walks all three:
    the mind–body problem the wager answers, the representational-computational picture it
    bets on, and the levels of explanation that keep the resulting sciences from talking
    past each other.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the founding wager, unpacked" widget="equationStep" config={cogsciCfg} />

  <h2>The mind–body problem</h2>
  <p>
    The oldest obstacle is conceptual: what <em>is</em> the relation between mental things
    (thoughts, feelings, that pink-ness of the evening sky) and physical things (neurons,
    voltages, chemistry)? René Descartes' seventeenth-century answer — two distinct
    substances, mind and matter, interacting in the pineal gland — made the problem sharp
    and bequeathed <em>dualism</em>, which still shapes every lay theory of mind. The
    modern problem survives Descartes in better form: if the mental <em>is</em> the
    physical (as almost all scientists assume — <em>physicalism</em>), why does it feel
    like anything at all, and how do the qualitative feels (<em>qualia</em>) arise from
    membrane voltages? David Chalmers' framing (1995) called the first questions — what
    does the mind <em>do</em>? — the "easy" problems, and this one — why is there
    experience? — the <em>hard problem</em>. The naming was rhetorical, but it drew the
    field's honest boundary: work on the easy problems is not evasion of the hard one,
    and progress on memory, perception and language proceeds whether or not anyone has
    explained why it feels like something.
  </p>
  <p>
    Cognitive science's characteristic move is <em>functionalism</em>: define mental
    states by what they <em>do</em> — their causes and effects, their role in the system —
    rather than by what they are made of. A memory is whatever plays memory's functional
    role; in principle, the same role could be played by neurons, silicon or (the
    thought experiment) water pipes. Functionalism is why the field can take computers
    seriously as models of mind without claiming brains are computers, and it is the
    philosophical licence for the whole enterprise: if mind is organisation rather than
    substance, then minds can be studied, built and compared across substrates. Alan
    Turing supplied the operational spirit in 1950 — replace "can machines think?" with a
    test you can actually administer (can its behaviour be told apart from a human's?) —
    and John Searle's Chinese Room argument (1980) supplied the standing objection:
    symbol-manipulation that passes tests might still lack understanding. The argument
    between them, refined but unresolved, is cognitive science's oldest running
    experiment.
  </p>

  <h2>Representation and computation</h2>
  <p>
    The positive theory of the wager has two clauses. Minds use
    <em>representations</em>: inner states that stand for things — the cat in your
    absence, the next word in this sentence, the location of your keys. And minds
    <em>compute</em> over those representations: they transform them according to rules
    or regularities — perceiving is transforming light into objects, remembering is
    encoding, storing and reconstructing, reasoning is chaining representations toward
    conclusions. The claim is not metaphor but mechanism: if you can specify the
    representations and the transformations precisely enough to build one, the theory
    becomes testable by running it.
  </p>
  <p>
    The evidence for inner representations comes from experiments that measure their
    shape indirectly, and the classic is mental rotation. Roger Shepard and Jacqueline
    Metzler (1971) showed people drawings of paired 3-D shapes — same object or mirror
    image? — rotated by various angles, and measured decision times. The result was a
    straight line: response time rose in lockstep with the angular difference, roughly a
    second for 180 degrees, as if participants were turning one image at a constant
    speed and comparing. Whatever a mental image is, it behaves like a spatial
    representation you operate on — a finding that launched a decade-long debate about
    whether imagery is truly picture-like, and a template for how the field argues:
    propose an inner representation, predict what operations on it should cost, and
    time the mind against the prediction.
  </p>
  <p>
    The twentieth century supplied the formal machinery. Turing's 1936 analysis defined
    computation itself — what any mechanical procedure can, in principle, do — and
    showed a single machine (the universal one) could perform any of it. Allen Newell
    and Herbert Simon drew the moral for minds in their <em>physical symbol system
    hypothesis</em> (1976): intelligence is, at root, the manipulation of symbols, in
    brains as in programs — and their theorem-proving and problem-solving programs were
    the first serious models of higher thought. The <em>connectionist</em> counter-movement
    (Rumelhart and McClelland's parallel distributed processing, 1986) objected that the
    brain's units are not symbols but simple neurons whose intelligence emerges from
    networks learning their own weights — the tradition that, scaled up seventy years
    later, became deep learning. Bayesian modelling offered the third grammar: the mind
    as an inference engine, updating prior beliefs with evidence the way statistics
    prescribes. And the modern large language models added an unexpected epilogue:
    artefacts trained on nothing but text that nonetheless display uncanny slices of
    language behaviour — now subjects of psychological study in their own right, and a
    live stress test of what each grammar of mind actually explains.
  </p>

  <h2>Levels of explanation</h2>
  <p>
    Why did you remember your password? Psychologically: it is in long-term memory,
    cued by the login screen's context. Biologically: synapses in your hippocampus
    strengthened through consolidation, a process that ran last night in sleep.
    Computationally: a retrieval process matched a key against stored entries and
    returned the value. Three descriptions, zero contradictions — and David Marr's great
    methodological insight (1982) was that this is structural, not coincidental. Any
    information-processing system can be explained at three levels: the
    <em>computational</em> (what problem is it solving, and why?), the
    <em>algorithmic</em> (what representations and steps does it use?), and the
    <em>implementational</em> (what physical machinery runs those steps?). Biology lives
    at the bottom level, psychology mostly at the middle, the theory of the task at the
    top.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> the levels compete — "a real explanation" must be
    neural, and psychological talk is loose talk. The levels are related like software,
    program and hardware: knowing the transistors does not give you the spreadsheet, and
    knowing the spreadsheet does not give you the transistors — but both, at their own
    level, are exact. The field's classic errors are level-crossings: "a memory is a
    synapse" (implementation mistaken for algorithm) or "the visual system computes
    because it is a computer program" (algorithm mistaken for implementation). Keeping
    the levels straight is the discipline's table manners, and most famous quarrels about
    mind dissolve into level confusions once parsed.
  </p>

  <h2>Models that run</h2>
  <p>
    What separates cognitive science from armchair philosophy of mind is executable
    theory: a model is a hypothesis written precisely enough to <em>fail</em>. The
    symbolic tradition built production systems — rules over symbols — that solved
    problems the way Newell and Simon argued humans do, even reproducing the
    errors; their General Problem Solver was evaluated against human protocols line by
    line. Connectionist networks showed that structured behaviour can be
    <em>learned</em> rather than programmed: trained on past-tense verbs or letter
    recognition, they made (tellingly) the same mistakes children make. Bayesian models
    formalised perception and reasoning as optimal inference under uncertainty, predicting
    not just behaviour but its rational structure. Each grammar captured something and
    missed something, and the argument between them — which representations, what learning,
    how much is innate — is the field's productive engine.
  </p>
  <p>
    The method has a characteristic rhythm, worth watching once. A behavioural experiment
    finds a regularity (say, people systematically judge some risks as more likely than
    their frequencies warrant). A modeller asks what representation and process would
    <em>produce</em> that regularity, and builds one — then tests it against the data it
    was built on, and against new data it has never seen. Neuroscience checks whether the
    brain plausibly implements such a process; comparative work checks whether other
    species share it; development checks how it comes to be. If the model reproduces the
    behaviour for the right reasons, the theory has survived one more test — and if it
    fails, the failure is usually more informative than the success, because it says the
    regularity's cause lies elsewhere. Every model is a wager on mechanism, cashable in
    public.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    The founding moment is worth knowing precisely, because the field's shape follows it.
    In 1956, within months: the Dartmouth workshop coined "artificial intelligence";
    George Miller published the magic number seven (working memory's capacity, and a
    manifesto for mental processes as measurable quantities); and Chomsky's work on
    formal language began the assault on behaviourism, completed by his 1959 review of
    Skinner — the argument that children produce sentences they have never heard cannot
    be explained by stimulus and response, and demands internal structure. By the late
    1970s the convergence had institutions: the Cognitive Science journal (1977) and the
    Cognitive Science Society (1979). "The cognitive revolution" was thus less a discovery
    than a permission slip: to talk about representations again, with methods.
  </p>
  <p>
    The core curriculum circles a fixed set of puzzles, each now a field: how perception
    builds a stable world from ambiguous, noisy input (the classic demonstration being
    that vision is inference, not photography); why attention is a bottleneck and what it
    selects; memory's systems (working versus long-term, and the multiple systems revealed
    by patients like H.M., who could learn skills he could not remember learning); how
    children acquire language from input too sparse for the grammar they acquire; and how
    people actually reason — including why the heuristics that serve us well also produce
    the systematic biases catalogued by the judgment-and-decision-making tradition. The
    open frontiers are the hard ones: the neuroscience of consciousness (global
    workspace and integrated-information theories now argue in adversarial
    collaborations), embodied and enacted cognition's challenge to the head-bound
    computer metaphor, and the question the LLMs force — which parts of mind ride on
    biology, and which are just information processing that any substrate will do. The
    wager is still open; the odds have never looked more interesting.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> MIT Brain &amp; Cognitive Sciences course sequence (OCW); Thagard, <em>Mind: Introduction to Cognitive Science</em>; Miller, "The Cognitive Revolution" (2003).</p>
